module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.setup_failure_substitutes_a_specimen_the_claim_still_admits

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data setup_failure_substitutes_a_specimen_the_claim_still_admits: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "setup_failure_substitutes_a_specimen_the_claim_still_admits" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a fixture step does not build what it was asked to, hands back a well-formed but DIFFERENT state, and the claim asserts its property of that substitute** (INVALID STATE: a green claim whose executed specimen is not the specimen its own setup describes.",

    "TWO PRODUCERS REACH IT AND THEY ARE NOT THE SAME DEFECT, which is the distinction the rest of this row is organised around. A step can OBSERVE ITS OWN REFUSAL and return the input anyway -- a success-shaped failure arm. Or a step can return SUCCESS having changed nothing, observing no failure at all. The first is a handling defect and the second is a silent no-op, and a repair for either leaves the other exactly where it stood.",

    "THE SUCCESS-SHAPED FAILURE ARM ALWAYS LOOKS CONSERVATIVE: a builder whose operation refused returns the repository, the tree, the config it was GIVEN. Nothing is fabricated, nothing is malformed, and the author's stated reason is that a broken fixture will surface as a failed claim. That reason is what needs checking rather than assuming -- returning the input is a second, silent success meaning `kept the previous state`, and whether the claim can tell that from `built the requested one` depends on the assertion, not on the arm.",

    "DISTINCT FROM `absorbing_fallback` BY WIDENING VERSUS SUBSTITUTION, which is the structural difference and is not a claim about which is easier to see: that arm answers with a SUPERSET of the subject it could not compute, so what it returns is not a member of the intended population at all. This arm returns a NEIGHBOURING MEMBER of the same shape, usually the state that existed one step earlier -- and a prefix of the intended specimen is exactly the state most likely to still satisfy an assertion written for the whole.",

    "WHY ASSERTION-ONLY REVIEW MISSES IT, stated at that scope rather than as invisibility in general: a reviewer who reads the setup PROSE and then the assertion finds them consistent, because the prose describes the intent accurately and the assertion is a correct statement about that intent. Nothing in the claim body is wrong. What is missing is any step establishing that the described state was REACHED, and that gap is in the setup path rather than in either text being compared. A review that traces the setup RESULT into the assertion sees it; a review that reads the two ends does not.",

    "**SPECIMEN (deep-carp-676, gunbc#10602, 2026-09-05), found by review and then reproduced by execution.** Two witness builders composed the SCM object store's insertion and the envelope's commit mint, returning their input repository when a step refused. The suite's three checkout controls built a two-commit specimen -- C1 freezing { a.dag }, C2 freezing { a.dag, b.dag } -- and checked out back to C1 to assert the candidate re-derives from the new base. With the insertion of b.dag skipped, the next mint still produced a DISTINCT commit over the UNCHANGED root, so C1 and C2 froze the same snapshot, the checkout had nothing to re-derive, and the assertion `one path, a.dag = A0, standing clean` was satisfied by a specimen that never contained b.dag. Measured: reintroducing that skipped step turns all three controls red under the repaired fixture and left all three GREEN under the old one.",

    "THE COMPANION DEFECT IN THE SAME SPECIMEN, because they are how the class hides: those controls also asserted the STANDING and the commit count after checkout without asserting the SELECTION. A checkout that returned its input unchanged is likewise standing clean on a corpus it can read -- it is simply standing on the wrong commit -- so the positive control for a refusal was satisfiable by a no-op. A substituted specimen and an under-specified assertion are separately survivable and jointly invisible.",

    "**RECOGNITION RULE: TRACE THE SETUP RESULT INTO THE ASSERTION. Neither question is answerable from the builder's TYPE, and an earlier writing of this row said the first one was.** It claimed that a builder whose type is the same on success and failure establishes that a step can fail without failing the claim. That inference is false in both directions, and this row's own repaired specimen refutes it: a builder there returns the previous repository, and the consuming comparison REJECTS it. Conversely a separate failure variant establishes nothing about the caller, which may match it and carry on. What decides the question is the path from the setup result to the assertion, so that path is what must be read.",

    "(1) FOLLOW EACH SETUP OUTCOME TO WHAT THE CLAIM DOES WITH IT. Where a step can produce a state other than the requested one -- by observed refusal or by silent no-op -- ask what the assertion returns on that state. A claim that admits it is a false-green PATH, and only tracing establishes that; a builder's signature is a hint about where to look. (2) NAME THE FACT THAT MAKES THIS SPECIMEN DISTINGUISHING, AND ASK WHETHER ANYTHING CHECKS IT. In the specimen above the discriminator is CONTENT -- the checkout can only be observed to re-derive if the two commit roots DIFFER -- and that scope is the specimen's, not checkout's: two commits with equal roots remain perfectly distinguishable by selected commit identity, and a claim asserting the selection would still discriminate. The question is which fact THIS claim rests on.",

    "**THE REPAIR IS TWO MECHANISMS AND NEITHER SUBSUMES THE OTHER.** A fallible builder -- setup failure as its own value carrying no subject, propagated through every later step and eliminated by every claim before it asserts -- prevents a repository from being carried INSIDE that failure value. It is producer-and-consumer handling that turns this into a rejected specimen rather than an accepted one, and the type is what makes that handling checkable at one site instead of at every call. Separately, asserting the specimen's own discriminating fact inside the builder catches the silent no-op, which observes no failure and so is reached by no failure vocabulary at all.",

    "WHAT THIS ROW'S OWN EVIDENCE ESTABLISHES, AND NO MORE. In the repaired SCM fixture both mechanisms are present and executed: setup failure propagates and is eliminated by every claim, the two-commit builder asserts its own differing-roots condition, and the skipped-step mutation is red where it was green. That is a bounded construction over one suite. It is NOT a proof that these two mechanisms exhaust the class, and this row does not claim a general attainable ceiling: whether a given repository can foreclose the defect depends on whether its setup paths and their consumers can be enumerated, which is a property of that harness rather than of the class. An earlier writing asserted structural impossibility here and a later one asserted mechanical preventability; both were numbers without derivations, and replacing a high one with a low one is not a repair. RUNG FOUND AT: outside the ladder -- the gap is invisible from the claim's own result, exactly as `check_subject_narrower_than_its_declared_claim` describes for a guard's range, and this row is its witness-grain sibling rather than a restatement: there the RANGE is too narrow, here the SPECIMEN is a different member. CEILING: UNDERIVED, stated as such rather than guessed, because DESIGN section 4b requires a ceiling to be DERIVED and a type name is not safety until its construction and acceptance boundary enforce the distinction.",

    "NEXT TRIGGER, NAMED AS THE ENFORCED CAPABILITY OVER A DECLARED POPULATION rather than as a type being available -- an earlier writing named `the witness harness supplying the fallible-construction vocabulary`, and supplying is not enforcing: a harness can offer the type while builders keep returning the input, producers misclassify a refusal as success, or consumers match the failure arm and carry on, all of which are expressible WITH the vocabulary. The capability is: over the witness population, a setup refusal cannot yield an accepted specimen or a passing claim, and a specimen's declared discriminating conditions are established before any behavioural assertion over it is credited. That is the boundary a trigger has to name; how it is realised is the builder's question, and nothing here obliges an SCM change to build it.",
    "**A THIRD PRODUCER, AND IT IS NEITHER OF THE TWO ABOVE: THE BUILDER SUCCEEDS COMPLETELY AND ITS DEFAULTS SUPPLY THE SUBSTITUTION** (gunbc#11193, 2026-09-14). No step refused and no step no-opped. A fixture helper built exactly what it was asked for, and the substitution entered through the components the CALLER DID NOT NAME -- the helper filled them from its own defaults, and those defaults belonged to a different subject. So the two producers this row is organised around are joined by one where nothing failed at all: the builder did its job correctly and the job was the wrong one.",
    "THE MECHANISM IS A COMPOSITE IDENTITY WITH INDEPENDENTLY SUPPLIED COMPONENTS. A serving decision identity is (field, evidence, quality requirement, policy, constraints, constraint authority, axes) -- DESIGN section 3d names them, and the receipt carries them precisely so a stale choice is detectable. The input builder takes all of them as separate arguments, so a fixture may pair one subject's FIELD with another subject's AXES and every component is individually well-typed. Substituting one component and assuming the rest travel with it produces a well-formed decision about a DIFFERENT question, which is this row's invalid state reached without any failure to observe.",
    "TWO ATTEMPTS, BOTH COMPILING CLEAN, AND THE DEFAULTS WERE INVISIBLE AT THE CALL SITE BOTH TIMES. First: `input_with(field:, w:)` reads as a field substitution and additionally supplies V4-Flash quality, constraints and axes; used with V4.1 candidates it built a decision under another subject axes. Second: candidates built by `cand(name:, art:, t:)`, which defaults `profile: serving_parameters_current` -- a V4-Flash execution profile under V4.1 axes that read V4.1 profile facts. Neither helper is misnamed and neither is wrong; both are convenient, which is exactly why they were reached for.",
    "WHAT THIS INSTANCE ADDS TO THE HARM ACCOUNT, STATED HONESTLY: the claim went RED, not green. The substituted decision did not happen to satisfy the assertion, so the false-green PATH this row is about was latent rather than realised, and the run refusing is the system working. It is filed because the path existed: had the other subject axes happened to yield the same arm -- and for a needs-evidence assertion that is entirely possible -- the claim would have passed while testing a different decision, which is receipt [5] one layer along.",
    "AND THE RECOGNITION RULE ABOVE WOULD HAVE CAUGHT IT, WHICH IS THE USEFUL PART. Tracing the setup result into the assertion means asking what the ASSERTION is about, and the assertion was about a V4.1 decision while the setup produced a V4-Flash one. What actually caught it was executing the claim. The check that is cheaper than either is available at the call site and was not applied: hold every component of the identity fixed except the one being changed, and read the helper to see which ones it fills in for you.",
    "REPAIR USED HERE, AND IT IS NOT A NEW MECHANISM: reuse a construction ANOTHER PASSING CLAIM ALREADY PROVES YIELDS THE ASSERTED ARM, rather than assembling an input from parts. `v4_1_input_for_runtime` is exercised by a separate claim that asserts its authorized-runtime input returns needs-evidence; leaning on it means the fixture is verified before it is depended on rather than after. A note was also written above the deleted helper recording that a third reader would reach for `input_with` for the same reason -- and a note protects one call site where this row protects the class.",
    "THE SAME CLASS SEEN FROM THE OTHER SIDE (2026-09-14, gunbc#11193 merging #11308), AND THE STALE COMPONENT WAS THE ONE THE AUTHOR HAD CHOSEN THEMSELVES. Warned that hour against substituting one component of a composite decision identity and assuming the rest travel with it, the author checked exactly that -- which FLEET SNAPSHOT the V4.1 field should be built from -- and leaned on the construction another passing claim proves, as the repair above prescribes. The component that had gone stale was the TOPOLOGIES: `v4_1_input_for_runtime` enumerated two hand-picked shapes, tp4 x 2 and tp8 x 1, BOTH consuming eight ranks, and #11308 had just made the host fit a HARD CONSTRAINT over an admissible set holding one fabric group. Both picks became unplaceable, so the field would have been emptied on HOST FIT before Pareto and the claim would have reached its expected needs-evidence verdict without ever consulting the runtime authorization it exists to exercise.",

    "WHAT THAT ADDS TO THIS ROW: the identity components an author must hold fixed include THE ONES THEY AUTHORED, and those are the ones that do not present themselves as assumptions. A helper defaulting a profile is felt as an input, so the recognition rule above reaches it; a topology list the author typed is felt as the fixture itself. The mechanical extension of the rule: when re-checking a composite identity, enumerate its components from the TYPE rather than from what the diff touched, because the untouched hand-written component is where the expired premise sits. Detection here was a textual merge conflict -- had the two changes not landed on the same argument, the merge would have been clean and the fixture would have survived intact and wrong. The class in its own right is `a_designed_narrowing_stops_inhabiting_the_subject_it_narrowed`; it is cross-referenced rather than duplicated, because that row is about the NARROWING going stale and this one is about the SUBSTITUTION that misses it.",

    "CEILING FOR THIS PRODUCER, ASKED RATHER THAN ASSUMED: it is decidable ONLY IF the pairing is declared somewhere. Today `serving_decision_input` accepts seven independent arguments and nothing states which axes belong to which subject -- the binding exists only in how the live input happens to be built. So no check can currently refuse a mismatched pairing, because there is no declared correct pairing to compare against. TRIGGER, as the capability: the decision SUBJECT owns its funded axes, constraint authority and quality requirement, so an input cannot pair one subject field with another subject axes and a fixture that tries is refused at construction. That is structurally guaranteed rather than impossible, since the components remain separately writable.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
